      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To investigate if complete resolution of endometriosis by laparoscopic surgery is beneficial to postoperative fecundity, dysmenorrhea and dyspareunia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "An observational comparative study on the outcome of laparoscopic surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "Laparoscopically-treated symptomatic women with endometriosis (total n = 236); complete (n = 185) and incomplete (n = 51) surgery groups."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MEASUREMENTS",
          "content": "Postoperative fecundity and symptom reduction."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "With whole populations, no surgical completeness-related difference was observed in cumulative pregnancy rates during the postoperative days 0-400 (cycle fecundity rate = 0.0319). Further accumulation of pregnant cases was followed in the complete surgery group (final cumulative pregnancy rate = 80%), but not in the counterpart group (p = 0.003). The similar result was obtained when only r-AFS classification stages III and IV were compared (p = 0.007). No r-AFS stage-related difference was observed in cumulative pregnancy rates when only patients of complete surgery were selected for comparison. The surgery reduced dysmenorrhea (84.7%) and dyspareunia (80.0%)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Laparoscopic conservative surgery for endometriosis, especially when it is complete, increases fecundity and reduces disease-related symptoms, such as dysmenorrhea and dyspareunia."
        }
      ]},
